00:51:41.953 --> 00:51:44.032
it's broken up into these things called row groups.

00:51:44.673 --> 00:51:46.112
Then it's broken up further

00:51:46.112 --> 00:51:48.193
into these things called column chunks.

00:51:48.193 --> 00:51:51.313
And then they're broken into smaller things called pages.

00:51:51.313 --> 00:51:53.872
And it doesn't really matter what each of those are,

00:51:53.872 --> 00:51:55.152
but the key thing to know is like,

00:51:55.152 --> 00:51:57.632
it's just smaller and smaller and smaller components.

00:51:57.632 --> 00:51:58.833
And the great thing about this is

00:51:58.833 --> 00:52:01.632
because the way Arrow is set up to read Parquet files,

00:52:01.632 --> 00:52:03.392
it can do things in parallel.

00:52:03.392 --> 00:52:05.632
So now instead of having to kind of go through

00:52:05.632 --> 00:52:07.473
all of this data in one big chunk,

00:52:07.473 --> 00:52:09.073
it can go through these individually.

00:52:09.632 --> 00:52:13.073
Now, Parquet files also store metadata

00:52:13.073 --> 00:52:17.392
about the row groups and the column chunks and the pages.

00:52:17.392 --> 00:52:19.473
So like if I'm trying to do some clever filter,

00:52:19.473 --> 00:52:22.593
like show me only values which aren't NA values,

00:52:23.232 --> 00:52:24.673
because I've got the metadata that says,

00:52:24.673 --> 00:52:28.752
okay, like this row group has like no NA values or something.

00:52:28.752 --> 00:52:30.433
It can just not even bother reading that

00:52:31.313 --> 00:52:32.193
when it's analyzing it.

00:52:32.193 --> 00:52:33.473
So it's all these little weird things

00:52:33.473 --> 00:52:35.553
about how it's subdivided that helps it kind of

00:52:35.553 --> 00:52:36.752
like be faster in that way.

00:52:36.752 --> 00:52:37.793
Yeah.

00:52:38.512 --> 00:52:39.152
Perfect.

