00:52:48.593 --> 00:52:51.953
It says, I have some data saved as a Parquet file.

00:52:51.953 --> 00:52:53.793
I'd like to load it with Arrow

00:52:53.793 --> 00:52:56.353
and then use pivot wider or pivot longer

00:52:56.353 --> 00:52:57.632
on it from dplyr.

00:52:57.632 --> 00:52:58.512
How would you do that?

00:52:58.512 --> 00:53:01.152
I resorted to DuckDB and then to Arrow,

00:53:01.152 --> 00:53:02.433
which seemed kind of clunky.

00:53:04.193 --> 00:53:05.313
Nope, that's a great way of doing it.

00:53:05.313 --> 00:53:08.433
So basically all of these dplyr functions

00:53:08.433 --> 00:53:10.272
that I was working with before kind of in Arrow,

00:53:10.272 --> 00:53:13.473
we've had to map them to the sort of underlying Arrow

00:53:13.473 --> 00:53:14.593
kind of code basically.

00:53:14.593 --> 00:53:16.032
And there aren't any mappings

00:53:16.032 --> 00:53:18.353
for pivot wider and pivot longer.

00:53:18.353 --> 00:53:20.193
So yeah, that approach is great.

00:53:20.193 --> 00:53:22.193
There's a function called toDuckDB,

00:53:22.193 --> 00:53:24.752
which basically lets DuckDB do that hard work

00:53:24.752 --> 00:53:26.112
and pass it back to Arrow.

00:53:26.112 --> 00:53:29.473
But because DuckDB understands Arrow internals,

00:53:29.473 --> 00:53:31.313
it means that we don't have to copy things backwards

00:53:31.313 --> 00:53:32.752
and forth and be slow.

00:53:32.752 --> 00:53:34.353
It just does it.

00:53:34.353 --> 00:53:36.032
So if the dataset is not something

00:53:36.032 --> 00:53:39.313
you just want to read in as like a table,

00:53:39.313 --> 00:53:41.632
then that is a fantastic way of solving that problem.

00:53:42.433 --> 00:53:42.833
Great.

